LABELS = {'colophon': 'The text used, and how it was established'}

COLOPHON = [
 '<b>The book.</b> The Han imperial bibliography enters “<i>The Methods of the Sima of Military Rites</i>, 155 chapters” not among the military writers but under the classics, in the section on rites. The <i>Records of the Historian</i>, in the life of Sima Rangju, says that King Wei of Qi ordered his officers to “go back over the military methods of the Sima of antiquity and set Rangju’s among them, calling the whole <i>The Military Methods of Sima Rangju</i>” — so the book is an old body of military ritual, into which the sayings of Tian Rangju, a commander under Duke Jing of Qi at the end of the Spring and Autumn period and Grand Marshal (<i>da sima</i>, whence his name), were later inserted. Of the 155 chapters five survive, and they are the five on this page: Benevolence the Root, The Duty of the Son of Heaven, Determining Rank, Strict Positioning, Employing Numbers — 3,420 characters of text. “Ascribed to Sima Rangju” is a librarian’s formula, not an attribution of authorship. When the Song set up a military curriculum in the Yuanfeng period, the book was made one of the <i>Seven Military Classics</i>.',

 '<b>The base text</b> is the punctuated <i>Sima fa</i> at Chinese Wikisource. Both the punctuation and the paragraphing are the base text’s: <b>this page has not divided a single passage of its own</b>. Neither of the two old witnesses is punctuated and neither is broken into paragraphs, so the paragraph breaks could come from nowhere else. Five chapters, sixty-eight passages.',

 '<b>Two witnesses, collated character by character.</b> The first is the <b><i>Sibu congkan</i> facsimile</b>: the Hanfen lou in Shanghai borrowed the Song-copied manuscript held by the Qu family of Changshu at their Tieqin Tongjian lou library and printed from it (so the imprint leaf says, adding the dimensions of the original block). It is the earliest line of transmission still in existence for this book. The second is the <b>Wenyuan ge manuscript of the <i>Complete Library of the Four Treasuries</i></b>. Both transcriptions come from Kanripo (KR3b0005, branches SBCK and WYG); the encoded rare graphs were resolved to Unicode against the KR-Gaiji character list, and the Siku text was checked a second time against an independent transcription at Chinese Wikisource. The machine collation yields <b>sixty-seven variant readings — twenty-two in the Sibu congkan text, forty-five in the Siku — spread over sixty-two character positions</b>. Differences of old graphic form and of interchangeable usage (德/徳, 歷/歴, 密/宻, 法/灋, 於/于 and the like) come to another two hundred and fifty-six, judged by four CJKVI variant tables and not counted as variants. Only pairs listed directly in those tables are accepted, with no transitive closure: they contain entries such as 上/二 and 丈/支, and a few steps of union-find pulls unrelated characters into one class.',

 '<b>How the text was emended.</b> There are three rules and no others: <b>(1) both witnesses agree and the base text will not construe; (2) both witnesses have a character the base text has dropped; (3) the base text has matter that neither witness has.</b> Outside these three, nothing is touched, and every remaining difference is recorded in the note on its passage. Six places were changed.',

 'One, chapter one, “to govern by rightness, and governing by it is uprightness”: both witnesses read “to govern by rightness is called uprightness,” and the base text repeats “govern by it” without construing. Two, the same chapter, “expedience issues from war, not from benevolence”: both witnesses read “not from ordinary men,” and expedience is precisely what benevolence does not yield. Three, chapter four, “manage the gag”: both witnesses read “hold the gag in the mouth” — the gag is a short stick held crosswise between the teeth to stop talking — and “manage the gag” is not Chinese. Four, chapter five, “the enemy halts, not the road”: both witnesses read “halts on the road.” Five, chapter three, “seventh, that the hundred officers wear nothing beyond their station”: both witnesses have a word meaning “properly” which the base text has dropped; <b>it is the only character added on this page</b>. Six, chapter one, after “then even war is permissible,” the base text carries eighteen characters — “In war: there is no eastern campaign in spring and no western campaign in autumn; at an eclipse of the moon the army is brought home; this is how war is spared” — which <b>neither witness has</b>. They are a fragment of the lost <i>Methods of the Sima</i>, quoted in the <i>Imperial Readings of the Taiping Era</i>, book 20, with the note that “weapons are of the shadow; at an eclipse the shadow is broken, so fighting ceases,” and Qing scholars collected the passage among the <i>Lost Fragments of the Methods of the Sima</i>. The words are the <i>Sima fa</i>’s, but they are not part of the five surviving chapters; the base text had moved them into chapter one, adding a lead-in and a final particle. They are removed here and recorded under their proper name.',

 '<b>Three places not emended, which the reader should see.</b> In each, only the Siku text supports the change, so by the rule above it was not made — and in each I think the Siku text is right. <b>One</b>, chapter one: “they did not press the helpless to the wall and pitied the wounded and the sick, and by this they made their <i>rightness</i> plain.” The base text and the Sibu congkan facsimile both have “rightness” twice in this passage, so that only five virtues can be counted where the text goes on to say “these six virtues.” The Siku text has “made their <i>benevolence</i> plain” in the first of the two, which completes the six — rites, benevolence, good faith, rightness, courage, wisdom — and answers the chapter’s own title, Benevolence the Root. <b>Two</b>, chapter two: “the Yin bore a tiger, which is white and martial,” between “honouring brightness” and “honouring pattern”; the Siku text reads “honouring awe,” which makes the three of a kind. <b>Three</b>, chapter four: seven drums are listed and then “the drums all together”; the Siku text reads “the <i>seven</i> drums all together.”',

 '<b>Two glyphs left unregularised.</b> 灋 is the archaic form of 法, “law.” This book uses both forms inside one sentence — “in an army, to keep the <i>law</i> (灋) in one’s own hands is called sole authority; to stand in awe of the <i>law</i> (法) together with those below is called <i>lawfulness</i> (法)” — and so do both old witnesses. It is the book’s own habit of writing, not a corruption, and it is left alone. 于 and 於, two graphs for the same preposition, are the same case: the base text uses 于 only in the sacrificial passage of chapter one, exactly as the Song-copied manuscript does. Two characters were in fact regularised: 皷 for 鼓 “drum,” which stands once against thirteen ordinary spellings, and the copyist’s form of 決 “decide” in chapter four. The two pairs of Western quotation marks in the base text have been changed to the Chinese kind.',

 '<b>Two things the collation turned up.</b> One: <b>the Siku text has no chapter title for <i>Employing Numbers</i></b>. Chapter four ends “such was the government of antiquity,” and chapter five’s first sentence runs straight on with no break at all. The Kanripo transcription and the Chinese Wikisource transcription of the Siku text are independent of one another and neither has the title, so this is not a transcriber’s slip but the edition itself; the title on this page comes from the Sibu congkan text. Two: <b>in the Kanripo transcription of the Sibu congkan text the scrolls are out of order</b> — the upper scroll is followed directly by the lower (Strict Positioning, Employing Numbers) and the middle scroll (Determining Rank) comes last. The head of each scroll states its own place, and the order has been restored from that.',

 '<b>A check, after the fact.</b> Once emended, this page’s text has exactly the same number of characters as the Song-copied manuscript in every chapter: 648, 942, 884, 681, 265 — 3,420 in all. The Siku text has one character more in chapter one and one more in chapter four.',

 '<b>The cover</b> is one of the witnesses’ own opening leaf: the <i>Sibu congkan</i> facsimile of the Song-copied <i>Sima fa</i> from the Qu family library at Changshu, scroll one, leaf one (Wikimedia Commons, Sibu Congkan 0343, page 138; public domain). The leaf carries, in order, “<i>Sima fa</i>, scroll one,” “Benevolence the Root, chapter one,” and the first sentence of the book, “in antiquity benevolence was taken as the root, and to govern by rightness was called uprightness,” with two vermilion collectors’ seals at the right. The block frame was measured by column and row luminance, a little paper left on each side, and the cut scaled to 560 pixels wide.',

 'The reading above is a complete translation, passage for passage, not a summary; the chapter headnotes and the parenthetical notes are this page’s own.',
]
